The fact-check system was already the first specialized MetaGraph.

For FACT_CHECK:

claim
  ↓
claim compiler
  ↓
typed DAG
  ├─ support search
  ├─ refute search
  ├─ primary-source search
  ├─ date/version check
  ├─ relation reversal check
  └─ numeric/unit check
        ↓
parallel fast retrieval
        ↓
Garak-style adversarial probes
        ↓
FactJudge
        ↓
verdict + evidence

For AGENT_TASK, you generalize the exact same runtime:

task
  ↓
task compiler
  ↓
choose graph/template
  ↓
typed DAG
  ├─ SEARCH
  ├─ EXTRACT
  ├─ COMPARE
  ├─ CALCULATE
  ├─ VERIFY
  └─ SYNTHESIZE
        ↓
cheap/fast executor per node
        ↓
deterministic/adversarial checks
        ↓
reducer
        ↓
completed result

The interesting realization is that FACT_CHECK becomes one graph template / one node primitive inside the broader system, rather than a completely separate architecture.

And this gives us a much better engineering plan: build the generic DAG runtime once, then plug in increasingly good graph programs.

For example:

FACT_CHECK
COMPARE_OPTIONS
INVESTIGATE
FIND_AND_VERIFY
RESEARCH_SYNTHESIS
TROUBLESHOOT

Each has a known topology instead of asking an LLM to invent a workflow every time.

Then Spark-class models only do narrow fuzzy operations:

classify task
generate 3 search queries
extract relevant claim
summarize evidence
fill schema

while code does:

dependency scheduling
parallel execution
deduplication
dates
numbers
ranking
thresholds
timeouts
retries

and the stronger intelligence only gets invoked when needed.

The Garak assault becomes particularly valuable because it gives every graph a cheap adversarial post-processing layer:

result produced
  ↓
what could make this wrong?
  ↓
relation reversal?
wrong entity?
stale date?
wrong number/unit?
missing competitor?
contradictory evidence?
unsupported conclusion?
  ↓
targeted extra node(s)
  ↓
accept / repair / escalate

So instead of a second expensive model saying “please critique this,” you're firing deterministic or tiny-model counterexample probes targeted to the actual output type.

That makes the central hypothesis very clean:

Reliable multi-step intelligence can be compiled into a DAG of cheap, fast, narrowly-scoped inference and deterministic verification, rather than requiring one large agent to reason through the entire task sequentially.

And the best part is that our FACT_CHECK work is no longer sunk cost at all. It becomes the first mature graph program for the general runtime.

