with the chosen confidence criterion.

And I'd make that gate slice-aware too. A policy cannot save money by quietly destroying one hard class:

overall accuracy       PASS
RELATION accuracy      PASS
NUMERIC accuracy       PASS
TEMPORAL accuracy      PASS
OOD / real claims      PASS
false-clear rate       PASS

That last one is particularly important. A system that becomes cheaper by prematurely saying “SUPPORTED” is useless even if aggregate accuracy happens to look acceptable.

So the true gate might be:

QUALITY GATE
├ overall ≥ baseline - ε
├ each critical phenotype ≥ floor
├ false-supported rate ≤ ceiling
├ insufficient/abstention calibration acceptable
└ secret/OOD set non-inferior

