And because you said cost first, then speed, the Pareto preference is explicit:

ACCURACY = constraint
COST     = primary optimization target
LATENCY  = secondary optimization target

That is actually very elegant for evolutionary search because most mutations die instantly at the quality gate.

Then the interesting mutations become things like:

remove redundant search
replace Tavily with structured API
change attack ordering
run two attacks concurrently
skip browser for this phenotype
reuse verified graph evidence
use smaller extraction model
stop after authoritative source agreement
change provider-specific query wording

If accuracy survives, keep the saving.

Over time you're effectively compressing the fact-checking computation while preserving behavior.

I think that's the right framing:

FactJudge defines the minimum acceptable epistemic quality. Cogym searches for the cheapest implementation that remains non-inferior, then minimizes latency within that feasible set.

That's much more scientifically defensible than “maximize some accuracy-cost-speed score.”